content.text:
All findings gathered. Here is the report.

## 1. Asset Browser delete UI

**There is no delete button/action anywhere in `apps/studio/src/components/asset-browser/asset-browser-panel.tsx`.**

I read the file in full (`apps/studio/src/components/asset-browser/asset-browser-panel.tsx:1-219`). The `AssetTile` component (lines 45-113, both grid and list variants) renders only a thumbnail, name, and type — it has `useDraggable` wiring for drag-to-timeline, but no button, icon, or context menu of any kind that calls `kernel.assetEditor.delete(...)`. A repo-wide grep confirms `assetEditor.delete` is never called from any `.ts`/`.tsx` file under `apps/studio/src` — the only place `delete` appears on that service is its own definition (`apps/studio/src/editor-kernel/services/asset-editor-service.ts:31-33`).

So "deleting an imported asset ... is not working" is explained most directly by point 1 alone: **there is currently no UI affordance to delete an asset from the Asset Browser at all.** No button, no keyboard shortcut, no right-click menu — nothing wires to `kernel.assetEditor.delete()`. This isn't a bug in error-handling/refresh logic, it's a missing feature/UI regression (or perhaps the user expected drag-to-delete or a keyboard `Delete` key while an asset tile is "selected", but there's no selection state on `AssetTile` either).

## 2. Delete call path (`AssetEditorService.delete` → `AssetManager.delete`)

- `AssetEditorService.delete()` (`apps/studio/src/editor-kernel/services/asset-editor-service.ts:31-33`) is a pure passthrough: `delete(assetId: AssetId): Promise<void> { return this.assetManager.delete(assetId); }` — it does **not** accept or forward a `force` option; there's no way to pass `force: true` through this service today.

- `AssetManager.delete()` (`packages/assets/src/asset-manager.ts:75-91`) confirmed exactly as recalled:
```ts
async delete(assetId: AssetId, options: { force?: boolean } = {}): Promise<void> {
  const references = this.dependencyGraph.getReferences(assetId);
  if (references.length > 0 && !options.force) {
    throw new Error(
      `AssetManager: cannot delete "${assetId}", still referenced by ${references.length} TrackItem(s)`,
    );
  }
  const entry = await this.options.catalog.get(assetId);
  await this.options.catalog.remove(assetId);
  if (entry) {
    await this.options.blobStore.delete(entry.contentHash);
    await this.options.thumbnailStore?.deleteAllForAsset(assetId);
    await this.options.waveformStore?.delete(assetId);
  }
  this.options.events?.emit("AssetDeleted", { assetId });
}
```
It throws synchronously-in-a-Promise (rejects) if `dependencyGraph.getReferences(assetId).length > 0` and `force` wasn't passed. Since `AssetEditorService.delete` never exposes/forwards `force`, **an asset browser delete call can never pass `force: true`** even in principle — moot anyway since no such call exists (point 1).

## 3. Error surfacing on delete

Moot in the strict sense because no delete call site exists (point 1), but confirmed for when one is added: the file has no toast/notification system visible anywhere (no toast import in `asset-browser-panel.tsx`; the only user-facing error handling pattern in this file is `handleImport`'s `try { await kernel.assetEditor.import(...) } catch (error) { console.error(...) }` at lines 148-160, which only logs to devtools console, no toast/UI feedback). If a delete handler were added following that same pattern (or worse, fire-and-forget with no `.catch()`), a rejection from `AssetManager.delete()` due to lingering references would produce **at most a console error, with zero visible UI feedback** — from the user's perspective, clicking delete would silently no-op. This matches the bug report's ambiguity ("no further detail") well.

## 4. List refresh after successful delete

Confirmed the refresh plumbing is correctly wired, assuming delete succeeds:
- `apps/studio/src/components/asset-browser/asset-browser-panel.tsx:124,131-142` — `entries` is refetched via `kernel.assetEditor.list()`/`listUnused()` inside a `useEffect` keyed on `[kernel, revision, unusedOnly]`, where `revision` comes from `useEngineRevisionStore`.
- `apps/studio/src/components/editor-kernel-provider.tsx:12-19` — `REVISION_EVENTS` includes `"AssetDeleted"` (alongside `CommandExecuted`, `CommandUndone`, `CommandRedone`, `AssetImported`, `PluginActivated`), and lines 57-70 subscribe to each of these on `kernel.appEngine.events` to call `bump()` on the revision store.
- `AssetManager.delete()` emits `this.options.events?.emit("AssetDeleted", { assetId })` only on the success path (`packages/assets/src/asset-manager.ts:90`), after catalog/blobStore/thumbnailStore/waveformStore cleanup — never on the thrown-error path.

So **yes**, if a delete call succeeds, the panel would auto-refresh via the revision bump → re-fetch. This confirms the refresh mechanism itself is not the bug; it's specifically that (a) there's no delete entry point in the UI, and (b) even if there were, a rejected delete (due to lingering references) would never reach the success path that emits `AssetDeleted`, so nothing would refresh nor would the user be told why.

## 5. Reference registration/unregistration — the real root cause once a delete UI exists

Confirmed exactly as suspected, and it's an intentional/known open gap, not just an oversight:

- **Registration on drag-drop**: `TimelineEditorService.addClipFromAsset()` (`apps/studio/src/editor-kernel/services/timeline-editor-service.ts:53-96`) bundles three commands into one `CompositeCommand`: `AddLayerCommand`, `AddTrackItemCommand`, and `RegisterAssetReferenceCommand` (lines 84-93). `RegisterAssetReferenceCommand.execute()` (`apps/studio/src/editor-kernel/commands/register-asset-reference-command.ts:22-24`) calls `assetManager.registerReference(assetId, trackItemId)`, which stores the reference in `AssetDependencyGraph` (`packages/assets/src/dependency-graph.ts:28-33`).

- **No unregister on TrackItem delete**: `TimelineEditorService.deleteSelection()` (`apps/studio/src/editor-kernel/services/timeline-editor-service.ts:119-130`) only dispatches `DeleteTrackItemCommand` instances. `DeleteTrackItemCommand` (`packages/timeline/src/commands/delete-track-item-command.ts:5-31`) removes the TrackItem from the `TimelineEngine` but **never calls `assetManager.unregisterReference(...)`**. This is explicitly called out in the doc comment right above `deleteSelection` (`apps/studio/src/editor-kernel/services/timeline-editor-service.ts:111-118`):
  > "Removes the selected TrackItems only. Does **not** cascade to the Layers they reference or any Animation clips on those Layers — ADR-010 ("does ripple-delete also remove linked items?") is still open, so this deliberately doesn't invent a cascade policy. The underlying Layer (and **its Asset Dependency Graph reference**) is left in place until that's resolved."

- `unregisterReference` is only ever invoked from `RegisterAssetReferenceCommand.undo()` (`apps/studio/src/editor-kernel/commands/register-asset-reference-command.ts:26-28`) — i.e., only when the original "add clip" composite command is *undone*, not when the resulting TrackItem is later deleted via `deleteSelection`.

- **Consequence**: once an asset has ever been dragged onto the Timeline (`addClipFromAsset`), its `AssetDependencyGraph` reference count never returns to zero via normal TrackItem deletion — only via undoing that specific add-clip action. So `AssetManager.getReferences(assetId).length > 0` stays true forever for that asset, meaning `AssetManager.delete()` will **always throw** "still referenced by N TrackItem(s)" unless `force: true` is passed — which, per point 2, `AssetEditorService.delete()` never supports passing anyway.

**Bottom line for the fix**: Two independent, compounding bugs/gaps explain "deleting an imported asset is not working":
1. `AssetBrowserPanel`/`AssetTile` has no delete UI at all wired to `kernel.assetEditor.delete()` — needs to be added (button + confirmation + error surfacing, e.g. a toast or inline message on catch).
2. Even once added, delete will spuriously fail for any asset ever placed on the Timeline, because `DeleteTrackItemCommand`/`TimelineEditorService.deleteSelection()` never calls `AssetManager.unregisterReference()` — this is a known, documented open item (ADR-010) that needs resolving (either cascade-unregister on TrackItem delete, or expose `force`/an "unused-only" gate with a clear "still in use" message in the UI) before delete can work as users expect.

All findings gathered. Here is the report.

## 1. Asset Browser delete UI

**There is no delete button/action anywhere in `apps/studio/src/components/asset-browser/asset-browser-panel.tsx`.**

I read the file in full (`apps/studio/src/components/asset-browser/asset-browser-panel.tsx:1-219`). The `AssetTile` component (lines 45-113, both grid and list variants) renders only a thumbnail, name, and type — it has `useDraggable` wiring for drag-to-timeline, but no button, icon, or context menu of any kind that calls `kernel.assetEditor.delete(...)`. A repo-wide grep confirms `assetEditor.delete` is never called from any `.ts`/`.tsx` file under `apps/studio/src` — the only place `delete` appears on that service is its own definition (`apps/studio/src/editor-kernel/services/asset-editor-service.ts:31-33`).

So "deleting an imported asset ... is not working" is explained most directly by point 1 alone: **there is currently no UI affordance to delete an asset from the Asset Browser at all.** No button, no keyboard shortcut, no right-click menu — nothing wires to `kernel.assetEditor.delete()`. This isn't a bug in error-handling/refresh logic, it's a missing feature/UI regression (or perhaps the user expected drag-to-delete or a keyboard `Delete` key while an asset tile is "selected", but there's no selection state on `AssetTile` either).

## 2. Delete call path (`AssetEditorService.delete` → `AssetManager.delete`)

- `AssetEditorService.delete()` (`apps/studio/src/editor-kernel/services/asset-editor-service.ts:31-33`) is a pure passthrough: `delete(assetId: AssetId): Promise<void> { return this.assetManager.delete(assetId); }` — it does **not** accept or forward a `force` option; there's no way to pass `force: true` through this service today.

- `AssetManager.delete()` (`packages/assets/src/asset-manager.ts:75-91`) confirmed exactly as recalled:
```ts
async delete(assetId: AssetId, options: { force?: boolean } = {}): Promise<void> {
  const references = this.dependencyGraph.getReferences(assetId);
  if (references.length > 0 && !options.force) {
    throw new Error(
      `AssetManager: cannot delete "${assetId}", still referenced by ${references.length} TrackItem(s)`,
    );
  }
  const entry = await this.options.catalog.get(assetId);
  await this.options.catalog.remove(assetId);
  if (entry) {
    await this.options.blobStore.delete(entry.contentHash);
    await this.options.thumbnailStore?.deleteAllForAsset(assetId);
    await this.options.waveformStore?.delete(assetId);
  }
  this.options.events?.emit("AssetDeleted", { assetId });
}
```
It throws synchronously-in-a-Promise (rejects) if `dependencyGraph.getReferences(assetId).length > 0` and `force` wasn't passed. Since `AssetEditorService.delete` never exposes/forwards `force`, **an asset browser delete call can never pass `force: true`** even in principle — moot anyway since no such call exists (point 1).

## 3. Error surfacing on delete

Moot in the strict sense because no delete call site exists (point 1), but confirmed for when one is added: the file has no toast/notification system visible anywhere (no toast import in `asset-browser-panel.tsx`; the only user-facing error handling pattern in this file is `handleImport`'s `try { await kernel.assetEditor.import(...) } catch (error) { console.error(...) }` at lines 148-160, which only logs to devtools console, no toast/UI feedback). If a delete handler were added following that same pattern (or worse, fire-and-forget with no `.catch()`), a rejection from `AssetManager.delete()` due to lingering references would produce **at most a console error, with zero visible UI feedback** — from the user's perspective, clicking delete would silently no-op. This matches the bug report's ambiguity ("no further detail") well.

## 4. List refresh after successful delete

Confirmed the refresh plumbing is correctly wired, assuming delete succeeds:
- `apps/studio/src/components/asset-browser/asset-browser-panel.tsx:124,131-142` — `entries` is refetched via `kernel.assetEditor.list()`/`listUnused()` inside a `useEffect` keyed on `[kernel, revision, unusedOnly]`, where `revision` comes from `useEngineRevisionStore`.
